---
import { PUBLICATIONS } from '../../data/publications';
import PublicationLayout from '../../layouts/PublicationLayout.astro';

const item = PUBLICATIONS.find(
  (entry) => entry.href === '/news/nomue-welch-limited-release-1/',
)!;

const toc = [
  { id: 'capability', label: 'What the release can do' },
  { id: 'research-agents', label: 'Why this matters for research agents' },
];
---

<PublicationLayout
  category={item.category}
  type={item.type}
  title={item.title}
  description={item.summary}
  published={item.date}
  status={item.status}
  toc={toc}
>
  <p>
    nomue's first agent-callable Scientific Verification Capability is now available in
    limited Release 1.
  </p>
  <p>
    Approved recipients can connect a compatible AI agent or HTTP client to nomue and
    use a hosted verification service for a defined statistical scope: independent
    two-group continuous data analyzed with a two-sided Welch two-sample t procedure.
  </p>
  <p>
    Public self-registration is not yet available, and access is currently limited to
    approved recipients.
  </p>

  <section id="capability">
    <h2>What the release can do</h2>
    <p>Limited Release 1 provides two verification paths.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Compute and verify</strong> allows an agent to submit data and the scientific
      information required for the supported Welch analysis. nomue checks whether the
      request falls within the supported scope and, when it does, runs the registered
      deterministic calculation and returns a structured result.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Verify a claimed result</strong> allows an agent to submit a claimed Welch
      result together with structured evidence. nomue then checks that claim against the
      registered execution and numerical rules.
    </p>
    <p>
      In both cases, nomue returns the result together with the relevant reasons,
      evidence, and suggested next action.
    </p>
    <p>
      If important scientific information is missing, nomue asks for clarification
      instead of filling in the missing meaning itself. Requests that are outside the
      supported scope are also kept separate from calculation or system failures.
    </p>
    <p>The capability is available through authenticated MCP and direct HTTP interfaces.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="research-agents">
    <h2>Why this matters for research agents</h2>
    <p>
      AI agents can help researchers organize tasks, interpret information, and
      coordinate workflows. Statistical verification, however, should not depend on an
      agent inventing missing experimental meaning or treating its own generated answer
      as the authority for a calculation.
    </p>
    <p>nomue provides a separate verification step for those bounded decisions.</p>
    <p>
      For supported requests, the numerical result comes from nomue's registered
      deterministic engine. When a request cannot proceed, the response indicates
      whether the agent should ask for more information, route the request elsewhere, or
      stop.
    </p>
    <p>
      This allows an AI agent to carry a scoped verification result back into a research
      workflow without confusing a successful API call with a successful scientific
      check.
    </p>
    <p>
      Limited Release 1 is the first operational release of this approach for nomue. It
      does not make claims about the relative performance of AI models; evaluation of
      frontier models remains a separate program.
    </p>
    <p>
      This release is limited to the defined Welch scope and does not assess the
      scientific validity of a study as a whole.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before release, the hosted capability was tested through its registered MCP and
      HTTP paths using synthetic fixtures. These checks covered both supported capability
      paths, authentication failures, credential replacement, service recovery, and the
      deployed runtime configuration.
    </p>
    <p>
      These are operational release checks. They are not an independent security audit
      and do not guarantee future service availability.
    </p>
    <p>
      Public documentation for the nomue Protocol and the locally runnable Release 1
      Record verifier remains available through the <a href="/docs/">nomue documentation</a>.
    </p>
    <p><a href="/nomue/">Compare the available nomue paths</a> · <a href="/roadmap/">Product and Protocol roadmap</a></p>
    <p>
      The hosted capability does not yet emit public Records for independent replay
      through the public Record verifier.
    </p>
  </section>
</PublicationLayout>
